      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Structured Data (SD-ELEMENT) Parsing: Multi-ID Bracket Syntax &amp; Escaping
      </h2>
      <p>
        Section 6.3 of RFC 5424 specifies the format for Structured Data (SD). An RFC 5424 syslog event may contain zero, one, or multiple SD-ELEMENT blocks. Each block is formatted as <code>[SD-ID PARAM-NAME="PARAM-VALUE"]</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Key structured data escaping rules and standard IANA profiles include:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>Character Escaping</strong>: Within parameter values, double quotes (<code>"</code>), backslashes (<code>\</code>), and closing brackets (<code>]</code>) must be escaped with a preceding backslash (e.g. <code>\"</code>, <code>\\</code>, <code>\]</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Private Enterprise Numbers (PEN)</strong>: Custom enterprise extensions must append the organization's IANA Private Enterprise Number separated by an <code>@</code> symbol (e.g. <code>[audit@32473 eventType="auth" user="alice"]</code>) to prevent namespace collision across vendor telemetry.</li>
        <li><strong>timeQuality SD-ID</strong>: The official IANA block providing cryptographic time synchronization context: <code>[timeQuality tzKnown="1" isSynced="1" syncAccuracy="60000"]</code>, indicating whether the local clock is synced via NTP and its synchronization error margin in microseconds.</li>
        <li><strong>origin SD-ID</strong>: Emits deep network source tracing: <code>[origin ip="10.244.0.15" enterpriseId="32473" software="envoy" swVersion="1.31.0"]</code>, establishing unambiguous provenance before NAT or ingress proxy traversal.</li>
        <li><strong>meta SD-ID</strong>: Transmits audit sequencing numbers: <code>[meta sequenceId="1048291" sysUpTime="842010" language="en-US"]</code>, allowing log collectors to detect missing or dropped UDP packets across transit hops.</li>
      </ul>

